Removed: NVIDIA EDM2 data preprocessing
---------------------------------------

Earlier revisions of this repository vendored ten files derived from
https://github.com/NVlabs/edm2, which is released under Creative Commons
Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 4.0:

    preprocessing.py
    Prior_only/preprocessing/dataset_tools.py
    Prior_only/preprocessing/encoders.py
    Prior_only/preprocessing/dnnlib/{__init__,util}.py
    Prior_only/preprocessing/torch_utils/{__init__,distributed,misc,
                                          persistence,training_stats}.py

CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 is not compatible with MIT in either direction: NonCommercial
forbids commercial use where MIT grants it explicitly, and ShareAlike requires
derivatives to stay under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. Relicensing was not an option, so
the files have been deleted and the functionality reimplemented from scratch:

    tools/image_prep.py        shared image discovery, labelling and cropping
    tools/build_image_h5.py    replaces preprocessing.py
    tools/build_image_dir.py   replaces dataset_tools.py convert
    tools/encode_latents.py    replaces dataset_tools.py encode and encoders.py

These are original work under the MIT license in LICENSE. They were written
against the output contracts this repository's own loaders impose --
CustomINH5Dataset and CustomH5Dataset in dataset.py, CustomDataset in
Prior_only/dataset.py, and sample_posterior in Prior_only/train_prior_only.py
-- and their latent-encoding loop follows this project's own VAE code in
models/autoencoder.py. Copyright does not reach the data layouts or the command
line those contracts describe, only the expression of the deleted files, and
none of that expression is reused here.

Two capabilities of the EDM2 originals were dropped rather than reimplemented,
because nothing in this repository used them: the pretrained-encoder zoo in
encoders.py (every recipe here encodes with this project's own VAE, so
tools/encode_latents.py accepts only --vae-type=e2e), and the dnnlib and
torch_utils support tree, which no code outside the deleted directory imported.


Components whose upstream licensing should be verified
------------------------------------------------------

These files carry a Meta copyright header whose text reads "licensed under the
license found in the LICENSE file in the root directory of this source tree".
That sentence was written for the tree the file originally shipped in; once
vendored here, the relative reference resolves to this repository's MIT
LICENSE, which is not the license the code was released under. Each file has
been annotated in place to say so.

They reached this repository through REPA and REPA-E, both of which are MIT
licensed and both of which already vendored these files and shipped the tree
as MIT. The licensing question is therefore inherited rather than introduced
here, but it is unresolved, and the original projects are non-commercial:

    generate.py                     via REPA, chain: DiT -> SiT -> REPA
    Prior_only/generate.py          via REPA, chain: DiT -> SiT -> REPA
    models/sit.py                   via REPA, chain: DiT -> SiT -> REPA
    Prior_only/models/sit.py        via REPA, chain: DiT -> SiT -> REPA
    models/mae_vit.py               facebookresearch/mae
    Prior_only/models/mae_vit.py    facebookresearch/mae
    models/mocov3_vit.py            facebookresearch/moco-v3
    Prior_only/models/mocov3_vit.py facebookresearch/moco-v3
    models/jepa.py                  facebookresearch/ijepa
    Prior_only/models/jepa.py       facebookresearch/ijepa

MAE, MoCo v3, I-JEPA and DiT are each distributed by Meta under Creative
Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0
(https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/legalcode).

The mae_vit, mocov3_vit and jepa files supply alternative REPA alignment
encoders only. Every recipe in this repository defaults to the timm DINOv2
encoder, so they can be deleted without affecting the documented results. The
generate.py and sit.py files are the sampling entry points and the backbone,
and cannot.

The two sit.py files had lost their copyright line somewhere upstream, leaving
a header that pointed at a license file without saying whose code it was. The
line has been restored to match the rest of the DiT-derived files here.


