Notes on source-fingerprint scan results
----------------------------------------

Every source file in this repository now carries a header naming its copyright
holder and license. Files without one were the root cause of most of the
findings below: with nothing local to read, a scanner falls back to whichever
project its fingerprint database matched, and reports that project's license
instead of the real one. Because the files vendored here have widely reused
ancestors, a scan of this repository reliably produces the matches below. They
are recorded here so the provenance does not have to be reconstructed on every
rescan.

This section covers the license findings. The vulnerability findings from the
same scans are assessed in docs/SECURITY_NOTES.md.

lumalabs/imm at e124a9d, reported as CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0
This match was correct about the license and wrong about the project. IMM
vendors utility code from NVlabs/EDM and says so in its README, and the files
it matched here were the EDM2-derived preprocessing files described in the
"Removed" section above. Those files have since been deleted and their
functionality rewritten, so a scan of the current tree should no longer produce
this finding. If it reappears, it is matching history rather than the working
tree; check which paths it names before dismissing it.

InvokeAI release-1.13, reported as GPL-3.0-or-later conflicting with MIT
Not a dependency, and not GPL. The scan's own metadata shows both.

The component is recorded with an empty build-dependency path and a match type
of "code snippet", so the tool agrees it reached this project by fingerprint
rather than through any dependency. The download it names is
github.com/lstein/stable-diffusion at release-1.13, a September 2022 snapshot
of the repository that was renamed to InvokeAI later that month.

The copyright holders the scan lists for that component are Lincoln D. Stein,
Machine Vision and Learning Group at LMU Munich, and Robin Rombach and Patrick
Esser. Those three names appear together in exactly one place: the MIT LICENSE
of lstein/stable-diffusion, which states that the code derives from CompVis and
carries the CompVis copyrights. The last two are the latent-diffusion authors,
and latent-diffusion is where models/autoencoder.py in this repository comes
from, as its header records. The chain is MIT to MIT.

The GPL-3.0-or-later verdict traces to the fourth copyright in the same list,
"Copyright (C) 2007 Free Software Foundation", which is the opening line of the
GPL-3 license text. That 2022 snapshot bundled a GPL-3 component and therefore
carried a copy of the GPL-3 text. The scan unions every license found anywhere
in the snapshot and applies the result to the matched snippet, but the snippet
is CompVis autoencoder code and no part of it is GPL. No copyleft obligation
follows.

xAR at 3b0bf91, reported against Prior_only/loss.py, no license
Not a dependency. xAR is another SiT descendant that also vendors REPA's
stochastic-interpolant loss, so a source-fingerprint scan of an earlier
revision of Prior_only/loss.py matched xAR's copy and then applied xAR's
"no license" status to this repository. The algorithm is still REPA's
(MIT, Prior_only/LICENSE); the file itself has been rewritten so it is no
longer a snippet of either xAR or the original REPA source, and carries an
SPDX MIT header. A later scan that still names xAR is matching ancestry,
not a copy.


Dependencies
------------

Dependencies are installed from PyPI rather than vendored, so their licenses
are not reproduced here. One is worth a note because scanners flag it: tqdm is
dual-licensed MPL-2.0 AND MIT. MPL-2.0 is file-level copyleft whose obligations
attach to distributing the MPL-covered files themselves, and it expressly
permits combining them into a larger work under other terms. Since tqdm is
installed as an ordinary dependency and no part of it is redistributed here, it
places no requirement on this repository.


Model weights and datasets
--------------------------

Nothing in this repository grants rights to the checkpoints or datasets it
downloads or reads. ImageNet, COCO, DINOv2, CLIP, SigLIP, the Flux VAE,
Qwen3-VL, the Cola-DLM weights and the GenEval and DPG-Bench assets are each
distributed under their own terms, several of which are research-only. Check
the terms of every artifact you fetch.
